; A canonical byte form for a parsed tree, and the SHA-256 digest of it.
;
; compiler/sha256.wtn settled that Witness can compute a hash, and compiler/parse.wtn
; settled that Witness can build a tree. This is the join: the bytes that stand between
; them. A digest is only ever a digest of bytes, so anything structured that is to be
; named by its hash needs one agreed spelling first, and that spelling -- not the hash
; function -- is where content addressing is either sound or quietly broken.
;
; WHAT THIS HASH IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
;
; This is a hash of the *written form*. It is not the hash witness/hashing.py computes,
; and the difference is not a detail.
;
; `witness/hashing.py` hashes the **checked** form of one definition. That is what makes
; a hash in this project name-independent: a parameter's name is replaced by its position
; in scope, a type parameter's name by its index, the arms of a `match` are sorted so
; that the order they were written in does not count, and every dependency appears as
; *its own hash* rather than as the name it was called by -- so the object is a Merkle
; hash over the definition's whole closure, and renaming a function, its parameters or
; its import aliases leaves every hash in the codebase unchanged. A contract, a
; redaction policy and a refinement are in there too, because they are part of what the
; definition does.
;
; This module can reproduce none of that, and says so rather than appearing to. Every
; one of those steps needs the checker: positions in scope need name resolution, a
; dependency's hash needs to know that a call head is a function rather than a variant or
; a builtin, and a policy needs the label a value carries. The checker does not exist in
; Witness yet -- compiler/typecheck.wtn is its first piece, and reads a type. So what is
; serialised here is the tree exactly as it was spelled: every name as the bytes of the
; token it came from, every list in the order it was written, nothing resolved and
; nothing dropped. Rename a parameter and this digest changes. It is therefore a
; **digest of source text in a canonical binary form**, useful for saying "these two
; sources are the same tree" and for a cache key, and it is *not* a content address.
;
; It is still the bottom half of the real thing rather than a detour. The real hash is
; the same two steps -- serialise a tree to bytes in one unambiguous spelling, then
; digest the bytes -- over a different tree: the checked one. When the checker exists in
; Witness, what changes is what goes in, not how it is written down or hashed. The
; marker at the front (`witness-tree-v0`) is there so that the two can never be mistaken
; for one another: a digest from this module is domain-separated from anything a later,
; name-independent serialisation will produce.
;
; WHAT THE SERIALISATION GUARANTEES
;
; One tree has exactly one spelling, and no two trees share a spelling. Both halves come
; from the same device: every variable-length part is preceded by a count, so the reader
; of the bytes never has to guess where a part ends from what follows it. The encoding
; is self-delimiting, and a self-delimiting encoding of a tree is injective -- which is
; the property a digest of it needs, and the one a naive `concat` of names and
; parentheses would not have, since `(ab)` and `(a b)` would run together.
;
; WHAT GOT IN THE WAY, FOR THE RECORD
;
; Six definitions are imported below and a seventh could not be. `tokens_of` -- the
; tokeniser, so that a digest could be taken of source text rather than of tokens handed
; over by hand -- cannot be imported alongside sha256's `digest`: resolution splices a
; dependency under the name the codebase holds it by, and compiler/reader.wtn's
; `reversed` and compiler/sha256.wtn's `reversed` are two different definitions with the
; same name, so bringing both closures into one module is refused with
; `[unresolved-import]`. A local alias does not help -- `(use ... as ...)` renames the
; definition asked for, not what it was built out of. So `main` and the propositions
; below hand the parser the tokens of a small form directly, which is what
; compiler/parse.wtn's own propositions do, and the tokeniser is reached through the
; codebase rather than from here. It is worth naming plainly: the one place this project
; still depends on a name is the one place it did not mean to.
;
;   witness test compiler/digest.wtn
;   witness run  compiler/digest.wtn --grant Alloc,Console
(module digest
  (use "wtn:ce6bc8d6e4773e0df82ceaa91e0c3827343653940f9f9a55fcce94111224ea10" as sha256)

  ; Hexadecimal, so a digest can be read and compared against one written down
  ; elsewhere. It is sha256.wtn's, by hash: a second spelling of the same sixteen digits
  ; would be a second thing to keep right.
  (use "wtn:90a50d84c8567129b33153303f56c5821e914f53aaabe33a6097595553c3817a" as hex)

  ; sha256.wtn calls this `length_suffix`, because there it writes the eight bytes of a
  ; message's bit length onto the end of a padded block. What it *is* is more general --
  ; the low `left` bytes of a word, most significant first, appended to what it is given
  ; -- and that is exactly a big-endian count prefix. A name is local, so this module
  ; calls it what it does here; the definition, and so its hash, is one and the same.
  (use "wtn:934b1382a831995f1867d308ac2dada8ebc9f3d79d5b7c9a56ce99ddf7ce1ca9" as big_endian)

  (use "wtn:86a99f8451d3c7998900c44d4eac8671aac7a339305b840140e9696becd82774" as Tree)

  (use "wtn:93b5829b146cf7fa9243f9e59065dc5c37d7f2b66bf5d70f9c359d5b870fe715" as parse)

  (use "wtn:7167847e8cee57f9c1c10d76cd9f09c0b6b9cedc0fff6a1af1c927ecc819bddd"
    as
    trees_count)

  ; --- counts ----------------------------------------------------------------
  ; A count is written in eight bytes, big-endian, always. A shorter encoding that chose
  ; its own width would need a rule saying which width a given number must use, and a
  ; serialisation whose canonicity rests on a rule nobody checks is the failure this
  ; module exists to avoid. Eight bytes is wasteful and has no edge cases.
  ;
  ; The guard is what supplies the bound `u64.of` needs. Nothing can reach the other
  ; branch: a count here is a number of bytes or of items held in memory, and no machine
  ; holds 2^64 of either. sha256.wtn's `bit_length` is guarded the same way and for the
  ; same reason -- saying so in an `if` costs no proof, and asserting it would.
  (fn wide ((n Int)) U64
    (requires (<= 0 n))
    (if (<= n 18446744073709551615) (u64.of n) (u64.of 0)))

  ; One byte saying which of the two shapes follows, then its count: for a leaf the
  ; number of bytes of text after the header, and for a list the number of items. The
  ; tags are 0x4c and 0x4e, 'L' and 'N', so that a hexdump of a serialised tree can be
  ; read by eye while this is still young enough to be read by eye.
  (fn header ((tag U8) (count Int)) Bytes
    (uses Alloc)
    (requires (<= 0 count))
    (bytes.concat (bytes.of tag) (big_endian (wide count) 8 (str.encode ""))))

  ; --- the tree, as bytes ----------------------------------------------------
  ; A leaf keeps the bytes of its token and nothing else, which is all the parser gave
  ; it: at this level `fn`, `30` and `"fn"` are each a run of bytes in a position. So a
  ; quoted string and the word its bytes spell serialise the same way, exactly as they
  ; parse the same way, and recording the difference would mean recording which kind of
  ; token it was -- which is a thing the tree does not carry and this pass must not
  ; invent.
  ;
  ; The two functions are a cycle, and every call around it takes a binder a `match`
  ; lifted out of the parameter: `items` out of a `Node`, and `head` and `rest` out of a
  ; `Trees`. A part of a part is smaller, so the walk needs no budget and no count of
  ; its own.
  (fn serialised ((t Tree)) Bytes
    (uses Alloc)
    (match t
      ((Leaf text quoted) (bytes.concat (header (u8.of 76) (unwrap (bytes.len text))) text))
      ((Node items) (bytes.concat (header (u8.of 78) (trees_count items)) (serialised_items items)))))

  (fn serialised_items ((ts Trees)) Bytes
    (uses Alloc)
    (match ts
      ((NoTrees) (str.encode ""))
      ((Trees head rest) (bytes.concat (serialised head) (serialised_items rest)))))

  ; The bytes that actually get hashed. The marker is a constant prefix, so it changes no
  ; distinction the serialisation makes; what it does is keep this digest from ever
  ; colliding with one taken over a different notion of what a definition is. A version
  ; number in it is cheap now and impossible to add later.
  (fn marked ((t Tree)) Bytes
    (uses Alloc)
    (bytes.concat (str.encode "witness-tree-v0") (serialised t)))

  (fn digest_of ((t Tree)) Bytes
    (uses Alloc)
    (sha256 (marked t)))

  (fn hex_of ((t Tree)) Str
    (uses Alloc)
    (let out (digest_of t) (hex out 0 (unwrap (bytes.len out)) "")))

  ; --- small trees, for saying things about ----------------------------------
  ; A tree cannot be written as a literal, and the propositions below need particular
  ; ones, so these five build them. They are also what the Python side calls: a function
  ; taking `Str` can be handed a made-up string from outside, where a function taking a
  ; `Tree` cannot.
  (fn leaf ((text Str)) Tree
    (uses Alloc)
    (Leaf (str.encode text) false))

  (fn single ((a Str)) Tree
    (uses Alloc)
    (Node (Trees (leaf a) (NoTrees))))

  (fn pair ((a Str) (b Str)) Tree
    (uses Alloc)
    (Node (Trees (leaf a) (Trees (leaf b) (NoTrees)))))

  (fn nested ((a Str) (b Str)) Tree
    (uses Alloc)
    (Node (Trees (leaf a) (Trees (single b) (NoTrees)))))

  (fn empty_node () Tree (Node (NoTrees)))

  ; --- what the serialisation says about itself ------------------------------
  ; The sizes first, because they are the whole of the encoding stated as arithmetic: a
  ; header is nine bytes, a leaf is a header and its text, and a list is a header and its
  ; items one after another. The marker adds fifteen.
  (test "a leaf is a tag, a count and its text" (= 10 (unwrap (bytes.len (serialised (leaf "a"))))))

  (test "a list of nothing is a tag and a count" (= 9 (unwrap (bytes.len (serialised (empty_node))))))

  (test "a list of two one-byte leaves is its header and both of them" (= 29 (unwrap (bytes.len (serialised (pair "a" "b"))))))

  ; Then the distinctions. Each of these is a pair of trees that a serialisation without
  ; counts, or without tags, would run together -- which is what "unambiguous" has to
  ; mean to be worth stating.
  (test "a leaf is not the one-item list that holds it" (not (bytes.eq (marked (leaf "a")) (marked (single "a")))))

  (test "where the lists nest is in the bytes" (not (bytes.eq (marked (pair "a" "b")) (marked (nested "a" "b")))))

  (test "the order of a list is in the bytes" (not (bytes.eq (marked (pair "a" "b")) (marked (pair "b" "a")))))

  ; The one a `concat` of names and spaces gets wrong: two leaves holding "ab" and
  ; nothing, against two holding "a" and "b". Both are two leaves whose text runs to
  ; "ab", and the counts are the only thing that tells them apart.
  (test "where the text is split between two leaves is in the bytes" (not (bytes.eq (marked (pair "ab" "")) (marked (pair "a" "b")))))

  (test "a digest is thirty-two bytes" (= 32 (unwrap (bytes.len (digest_of (empty_node))))))

  (property "the same tree always gives the same bytes" ((text Str))
    (bytes.eq (marked (leaf text)) (marked (leaf text))))

  (property "different text in a leaf gives different bytes" ((a Str) (b Str))
    (requires (not (str.eq a b)))
    (not (bytes.eq (marked (leaf a)) (marked (leaf b)))))

  (property "a leaf is never the list that holds it" ((text Str))
    (not (bytes.eq (marked (leaf text)) (marked (single text)))))

  (property "the order of two leaves is in the bytes" ((a Str) (b Str))
    (requires (not (str.eq a b)))
    (not (bytes.eq (marked (pair a b)) (marked (pair b a)))))

  ; --- a form read and then digested -----------------------------------------
  ; `(+ a b)` as the reader would have left it. Written out rather than tokenised,
  ; because the tokeniser cannot be imported here; see the note at the top of the file.
  (fn sample_tokens () Tokens
    (uses Alloc)
    (Tokens (Open)
      (Tokens (Word (str.encode "+"))
        (Tokens (Word (str.encode "a"))
          (Tokens (Word (str.encode "b")) (Tokens (Close) (NoTokens)))))))

  (fn sample_digest () Str
    (uses Alloc)
    (match (parse (sample_tokens))
      ((Ok t) (hex_of t))
      ((Err f) (concat "rejected: " (get f why)))))

  ; Not a published vector -- there is nothing published to check this against, and
  ; saying there were would be the dishonesty this module's comments exist to avoid.
  ; What it is: the digest this code gave when it was written down, so that a change in
  ; the serialisation cannot slip through as a change in nothing. tests/test_digest.py is
  ; where the digest is checked against an independent SHA-256 of the same bytes.
  (snapshot "the digest of (+ a b)" (sample_digest)
    "9773115c5f049eef0711c73f82fbd39941e8186d09ded2dcbbb8035ec47abe11")

  (fn main () Unit
    (uses Alloc Console)
    (seq (print (sample_digest)) (print (hex_of (empty_node))))))
